r pieces that we do  they'll say one thing to me on the phone  then I say can we get that on camera and  they clam up  you've in talking with you previously I  know you've come across quite a lot of  this what is sort of the unsaid that we  won't see I hear CEOs the common the  common thread is they're afraid to say  certain things on camera that they think  would put them in a position to be  targeted by bureaucrats targeted by you  the state you know the state government  or the federal administration things  like speaking to unkindly about specific  provisions in Obamacare speaking to  unkindly about specific regulations in  the state of California I've had a few  CEOs say that they didn't want to do the  series because they thought that it made  them too vulnerable it's pretty sad when  CEOs in our country business leaders job  creators in our country are afraid of  what they might say and have a  government come come down on them are  there things that specific things that  they're really worried about yeah you  the most interesting interview and it  was kind of the inspiration for for the  series and and and and continuing on in  the series was the interview with Scott  McGregor who's the CEO of Broadcom and  he actually outlined three areas where  he thought that government could do  things a little bit differently that  would help his his business obviously  which is something that a lot of  California CEOs talk about which is the  tax code in California the tax code in  California is egregious compared to many  other states and he said that reforming  the corporate tax code or at least  bringing it in line with other states  would be very helpful in terms of them  being able to hire more and to use more  of their capital towards investment and  innovation which would be critical for  for for a technology company the second  thing which I think is the most  interesting thing that's come out of the  CEO solution series so far was when he  told me that one sim